      "narration": "Key-based authentication is worth understanding rather than just following the steps, because the mechanism is genuinely clever and knowing it makes the security properties obvious. You generate a pair of mathematically related keys. The private one stays on your machine forever. The public one you copy to any server you want to reach. When you connect, the server does not ask for your key. It sends your client a random challenge, your client signs that challenge using the private key, and the server verifies the signature against the public key it already has. Your private key is never transmitted, not even in encrypted form. Sit with what that gives you: even if a server you connect to is completely compromised, the attacker learns nothing they can use to impersonate you elsewhere, because they only ever saw a signature over a random value. Compare that with a password, which you hand over in full to every server you log into. That is why key authentication is not merely more convenient than passwords — it is a fundamentally stronger design. Use ed25519 rather than RSA: the keys are much shorter, verification is faster, and there is no key-size decision you can get wrong. And remember from the permissions lesson that SSH refuses a private key others can read, so chmod six zero zero.",
